  "prompt": "When was the first remote control tv invented?",
  "task_completions": [
    "The story of the first remote control for TV is a bit more complex than a single invention date! Here's a breakdown:\n\n* **1950: Zenith Radio Corporation's \"Lazy Bones\"** - This is generally considered the *first* commercially available TV remote control. It wasn't wireless, though! It used a cable connecting the remote to the television set.  It allowed you to change channels and adjust the volume without getting up.\n* **1955: Zenith's \"Flash-Matic\"** - This was the *first* wireless remote control. It used a beam of light aimed at photoelectric cells on the TV.  However, it had problems! Sunlight and bright indoor lights could interfere with it, and it wasn't very reliable.\n* **1956: Zenith's \"Space Command\"** - This is the remote most people consider the real breakthrough. It used *ultrasonic* sound to change channels.  It was much more reliable than the Flash-Matic, and became very popular.  It used different clicking sounds for different functions.  This technology dominated for the next 25 years.\n* **1980s: Infrared (IR) remotes** - Infrared remotes, like the ones we largely use today, began to replace ultrasonic remotes in the 1980s. They were less susceptible to interference and offered more functionality.\n\n\n\n**So, to answer your question directly, while the first *remote control* was in 1950, the first *wireless* remote control was in 1955.**  However, the first truly *successful* and widely adopted wireless remote was the Zenith Space Command in 1956.\n\n\n\nYou can find more details here:\n\n*   **Smithsonian Magazine:** [https://www.smithsonianmag.com/innovation/the-history-of-the-tv-remote-control-180976655/](https://www.smithsonianmag.com/innovation/the-history-of-the-tv-remote-control-180976655/)\n*   **HowStuffWorks:** [https://electronics.howstuffworks.com/tv-remote-control.htm](https://electronics.howstuffworks.com/tv-remote-control.htm)",
    "The history of the TV remote control is a bit more complex than a single \"first\" invention! Here's a breakdown of the key milestones:\n\n* **1950: Zenith Radio Corporation's \"Lazy Bones\"** - This is generally considered the *first* commercially available TV remote control. However, it wasn't wireless! It used a cable connecting the viewer's chair to the TV. It allowed viewers to change channels and adjust volume without getting up. It was invented by Eugene Polley.\n\n* **1955: Zenith's \"Flash-Matic\"** - This was the *first* wireless remote control. It used a beam of light aimed at photoelectric cells on the TV to change channels.  It had some problems - sunlight or bright lamps could interfere with it, and it only changed channels (no volume control). It was invented by Robert Adler.\n\n* **1956: Zenith's \"Space Command\"** - This is the remote most people recognize as the ancestor of modern remotes. It used *ultrasonic* sound – clicking buttons created different frequencies that the TV responded to. It was much more reliable than the Flash-Matic and became incredibly popular.  Also invented by Robert Adler, it was a significant improvement and the basis for remotes for the next 25 years.\n\n* **1980s: Infrared Remotes** - Infrared remotes, which are what we largely use today, began to replace ultrasonic remotes in the 1980s. They were less susceptible to interference and more efficient.\n\n\n\n**Therefore, while the \"Lazy Bones\" in 1950 was the first to offer remote control functionality, the first *wireless* remote control was the \"Flash-Matic\" in 1955. However, the \"Space Command\" in 1956 was the first *practical* and widely adopted wireless remote.**\n\n\n\nYou can find more detailed information here:\n\n*   **Smithsonian Magazine:** [https://www.smithsonianmag.com/innovation/the-history-of-the-tv-remote-control-180976668/](https://www.smithsonianmag.com/innovation/the-history-of-the-tv-remote-control-180976668/)\n*   **HowStuffWorks:** [https://electronics.howstuffworks.com/remote-control.",
    "The history of the TV remote control is surprisingly long and evolved over time! Here's a breakdown of the key milestones:\n\n* **1950: Zenith Radio Corporation's \"Lazy Bones\"** - This is generally considered the *first* TV remote control available to the public. However, it wasn't wireless! It used a cable connecting the remote to the TV. It allowed you to change channels and adjust the volume without getting up.\n* **1955: Zenith's \"Flash-Matic\"** - This was the *first* wireless remote control! It used a beam of light aimed at photo cells on the TV.  However, it had problems.  Sunlight or bright lamps could interfere with the signal, and it wasn't very reliable.\n* **1956: Zenith's \"Space Command\"** - This is the remote most people recognize as the ancestor of modern remotes. It used *ultrasonic* sound – essentially, it clicked, and the TV responded to the specific clicks. It was much more reliable than the Flash-Matic, and became quite popular.  It remained the dominant technology for decades.\n* **1980: Zenith introduces the first infrared remote control.** While ultrasonic remotes were popular, they had issues with interference. Infrared technology proved more reliable and is the basis for most remotes used today.\n\n**So, to answer your question directly: the first *wireless* remote control was the Zenith Flash-Matic in 1955.** However, the first remote control *period* was the wired \"Lazy Bones\" in 1950.\n\n\n\nYou can find more information at these links:\n\n*   **Smithsonian Magazine:** [https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/how-the-tv-remote-control-changed-our-lives-180976668/](https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/how-the-tv-remote-control-changed-our-lives-180976668/)\n*   **HowStuffWorks:** [https://electronics.howstuffworks.com/remote-control.htm](https://electronics.howstuffworks.com/remote-control.htm)\n*   **Wikipedia:** [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Remote_control](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Remote_control)"
  ],
